{
  "dom_id": "step-q7006",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$",
